 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "To save a notebook as a usable Wolfram Language context/package file for \
AI systems, the usual path is:\n\n## Best option: save as a package file\n\n\
1. Put your reusable definitions in initialization/package style cells.\n2. \
In the notebook, use:\n   **File \[FilledRightTriangle] Save As Special \
\[FilledRightTriangle] Package Format**\n3. Save it as a ``.wl`` or ``.m`` \
file.\n\nThat gives you a plain-text source file your AI tooling can load \
with:\n\n```wl\nGet[\"path/to/MyContext.wl\"]\n```\n\nor\n\n```wl\n\
Needs[\"MyContext`\"]\n```\n\nif it is on ``$Path`` and structured as a \
package.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended package structure\n\nA minimal context file \
looks like this:\n\n```wl\nBeginPackage[\"RHUFT`\"]\n\n\
temporalCoherence::usage = \"temporalCoherence[state1, state2] gives a \
normalized similarity score.\";\ndriftMetric::usage = \"driftMetric[state, \
ideal] gives squared deviation from an ideal state.\";\nmemoryStep::usage = \
\"memoryStep[state, memory, input, alpha, gamma, delta, lambda] advances the \
toy memory model.\";\nreflectionStep::usage = \"reflectionStep[state, memory, \
input, alpha, zeta, lambda] advances the reflection model.\";\n\n\
Begin[\"`Private`\"]\n\ntemporalCoherence[state1_, state2_] := \n  Abs[state1 \
. Conjugate[state2]]/(Norm[state1] Norm[state2] + 10^-12)\n\n\
driftMetric[state_, ideal_] := Norm[state - ideal]^2\n\nmemoryStep[state_, \
memory_, input_, alpha_, gamma_, delta_, lambda_] := \n  Module[{nextState, \
nextMemory},\n    nextState = alpha state + gamma input + delta memory;\n    \
nextMemory = (1 - lambda) memory + lambda state;\n    <|\"State\" -> \
nextState, \"Memory\" -> nextMemory|>\n  ]\n\nreflectionStep[state_, memory_, \
input_, alpha_, zeta_, lambda_] := \n  Module[{predicted, nextState, \
nextMemory},\n    predicted = (state + memory)/2;\n    nextState = alpha \
state + zeta (predicted - state) + input;\n    nextMemory = (1 - lambda) \
memory + lambda state;\n    <|\"State\" -> nextState, \"Memory\" -> \
nextMemory, \"Predicted\" -> predicted|>\n  ]\n\nEnd[]\nEndPackage[]\n```\n\n\
---\n\n## How to use it later\n\nIf saved as ``RHUFT.wl``:\n\n```wl\n\
Get[\"/full/path/RHUFT.wl\"]\n```\n\nThen call:\n\n```wl\n\
RHUFT`memoryStep[1.0, 0.5, 0.1, 0.8, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1]\n```\n\nIf you want \
``Needs`` support, place the file somewhere on ``$Path`` or add its \
directory:\n\n```wl\nAppendTo[$Path, \"/full/path/to/package/folder\"]\n\
Needs[\"RHUFT`\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## If your notebook contains text + code\n\n\
If it is a research notebook with prose, derivations, and examples, don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t rely on raw notebook saving alone for AI-system loading.\n\
\nInstead:\n\n- keep the notebook as your human-readable source\n- export a \
clean ``.wl`` package containing only executable definitions\n- optionally \
export a separate markdown/plaintext knowledge file for the AI\n\nA good \
split is:\n\n- ``RHUFT.nb`` \[LongDash] full notebook\n- ``RHUFT.wl`` \
\[LongDash] executable package/context\n- ``RHUFT.md`` \[LongDash] conceptual \
documentation for the AI system\n\n---\n\n## Important note about notebooks\n\
\nA ``.nb`` file is not usually the best direct \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]context file\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] for automated \
loading.  \nFor programmatic reuse, a package file is much better than trying \
to parse notebook cells.\n\nUse notebooks for:\n- exploration\n- \
documentation\n- interactive testing\n\nUse ``.wl`` packages for:\n- contexts\
\n- reusable symbols\n- loading into agents, scripts, kernels, and APIs\n\n\
---\n\n## If you want initialization cells only\n\nAnother workflow is:\n\n1. \
Mark definition cells as **Initialization Cells**\n2. Save notebook\n3. Use \
[NotebookEvaluate](paclet:ref/NotebookEvaluate) to load definitions from the \
notebook\n\nExample:\n\n```wl\nNotebookEvaluate[\"/full/path/RHUFT.nb\"]\n```\
\n\nThis works, but for AI systems a ``.wl`` package is still cleaner and \
more robust.\n\n---\n\n## Recommendation for your AI setup\n\nFor your use \
case, I\[CloseCurlyQuote]d suggest:\n\n1. create a package context like \
``RHUFT``\n2. put all core operators, metrics, and simulation functions in \
``RHUFT.wl``\n3. keep ontology notes and test plan in the notebook\n4. \
optionally create an association-based registry such as:\n\n```wl\n\
RHUFT`$CoreDefinitions = <|\n  \"TemporalCoherence\" -> temporalCoherence,\n  \
\"DriftMetric\" -> driftMetric,\n  \"MemoryStep\" -> memoryStep,\n  \
\"ReflectionStep\" -> reflectionStep\n|>;\n```\n\nThat makes it easier for \
external AI orchestration code to inspect available functions.\n\n---\n\n## \
Minimal command-line style save route\n\nIf the definitions are already \
loaded in a kernel, you can also write a context file manually with \
[Save](paclet:ref/Save):\n\n```wl\nSave[\"/full/path/RHUFT.wl\", \
{RHUFT`temporalCoherence, RHUFT`driftMetric, RHUFT`memoryStep, \
RHUFT`reflectionStep}]\n```\n\nBut hand-written package files are usually \
better organized than auto-saved output.\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can turn \
your RHUFT notebook material into a clean ``RHUFT` `` package skeleton with:\n\
- public symbols\n- usage messages\n- private implementation\n- test helpers\n\
- simulation functions", "ChatData" -> 
